! ZM's two terms: the normalization modifier and the exponent bound.
!
! HOW THESE WERE FOUND.  mutation_score.py's zm-drops-normalized-term
! and zm-exponent-bound both SURVIVED the whole suite.
!
! ZM (indicator 33, ZERO MULTIPLY) is set by fpMul as
!
!     normalized && zero && (e > -1024)
!
! -- Reference Manual p.99.  jb_af1c F214D6 is the first and only test
! of ZM anywhere in the transcribed diagnostic, and it pins the
! indicator's EXISTENCE, not the two qualifying terms.
!
! THE NORMALIZED TERM was already characterised in the plan doc, after
! an injection that was "wrong in the most useful way yet": the only
! multiply in F214 where dropping `normalized` changes anything is C1
! (FPK0 x FPK56, unflagged, zero product, unnormalized) -- and C1 checks
! REGISTERS, not indicators.  So nothing in F214 discriminates it.  The
! mutation campaign rediscovered that independently.
!
! THE EXPONENT BOUND is new: p.99 says GREATER THAN -1024, and nothing
! transcribed lands a zero product ON the boundary, so > and >= are
! indistinguishable.
!
! THE CONSTANTS are built from the format directly (StretchFP's
! fpFromWord: ef at bit 63, em at 62-53, es at 52, fraction at 51-4), because
! the pool has nothing with a zero fraction at a chosen exponent:
!
!   ZF0     fraction 0, exponent 0
!   NZF0    fraction 2**47, exponent 0
!   ZF512   fraction 0, exponent -512
!   NZF512  fraction 2**47, exponent -512
!
! A multiply by a zero-fraction operand gives a zero product whose
! exponent is the SUM, so ZF0 x NZF0 lands at 0 and ZF512 x NZF512 lands
! at exactly -1024, the boundary itself.
!
! CASE 1 IS A POSITIVE CONTROL and it is not optional: without it, a
! model that never set ZM at all would pass both off-checks and the
! program would prove nothing.  That is the same trap the index
! indicators were in before index_indicators_off existed.
!
! ONE TRAP, ALREADY ON RECORD AND WALKED INTO ANYWAY: a bare `*` is
! AMBIGUOUS and assembles as a 64-bit VFL instruction (op 0x80), not a
! floating-point multiply -- exactly as a bare `L` does.  The normalized
! multiply has to be written `*(N)`.  The first version of this program
! used `*` and failed in a way that looked like a model bug.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, STATED: hand-written from p.99, not transcribed.

        SLC, (8)3200.0
!
! ---- case 1: normalized, zero product, exponent 0 -> ZM ON ---------
START   Z, $IND                      ! ZM is bit 33, so Z,$IND clears it
        L(U), ZF0                    ! accumulator := fraction 0, exponent 0
        *(N), NZF0                   ! NORMALIZED multiply, zero product
        BZB, $ZM, $                  ! hang up if ZM off -- the control
!
! ---- case 2: UNNORMALIZED -> ZM OFF -------------------------------
        Z, $IND
        L(U), ZF0
        *(U), NZF0                   ! same operands, unnormalized
        BB, $ZM, $                   ! hang up if ZM on -- `normalized` is
                                     !   false, so the term must gate it
!
! ---- case 3: exponent exactly -1024 -> ZM OFF ---------------------
        Z, $IND
        L(U), ZF512                  ! exponent -512
        *(N), NZF512                 ! -512 + -512 = -1024, the boundary
        BB, $ZM, $                   ! hang up if ZM on -- p.99 says
                                     !   GREATER than -1024, not >=
        B, PASS
!
ZF0     DD(BU,64), 0                 ! fraction 0, exponent 0
NZF0    DD(BU,64), 2251799813685248  ! fraction 2**47, exponent 0
ZF512   DD(BU,64), 4616189618054758400   ! fraction 0, exponent -512
NZF512  DD(BU,64), 4618441417868443648   ! fraction 2**47, exponent -512
PASS    BEW, $
        END, START
